out: value: bool:true
class: normative
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-numerictorawbytes spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-rawbytestonumeric ref:numericsref@1
script: (function () { var v = new DataView(new ArrayBuffer(2)); v.setFloat16(0, $f64(0x7FF000FF, 0xC0000000)); var b = v.getUint16(0); return (b & 0x7C00) === 0x7C00 && (b & 0x3FF) !== 0; })()
note: a NaN is written as some binary16 NaN encoding; which one is the implementation's (NumericToRawBytes step 1.b), and encode-nan-bits vectors pin each engine's choice
since: 6

id: numerics/encode-nan-bits/0001
area: numerics
op: Float16 encode
in: str:"NaN 7FF8000000000000"
out: value: f64:0x40DF800000000000
class: impl-defined
per-engine.broiler-js: value: f64:0x40DF800000000000
per-engine.broiler-vm: value: f64:0x40DF800000000000
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-numerictorawbytes ref:numericsref@1
script: (function () { var v = new DataView(new ArrayBuffer(2)); v.setFloat16(0, $f64(0x7FF80000, 0x00000000)); return v.getUint16(0); })()
note: the encoding of a NaN is the implementation's; out holds the canonical quiet NaN 0x7E00. The double NaN is built from its bits, and an engine that keeps a NaN's payload keeps it to the write. Broiler.VM writes one canonical NaN (JsFloat16.QuietNaN); Broiler.JS keeps the sign and the ten most significant bits of the payload and quiets it (SharedLibraries.md, SJL-4.2). The shared Float16.Encode takes the policy as a parameter (JSD-0067 decision 5)
since: 6

